# /usr/bin/shadowfetch-welcome imports /usr/lib/shadowfetch/desktop/sf_desktop.py
# -- the one shared desktop library (W-30) -- for the bundle catalog, the
# hwscan freshness rule, the five helper paths and the privileged install
# argv. It raises ImportError without it, on purpose: a wizard that silently
# shows an empty catalog is worse than one that says the install is broken.
# So this is Depends and not Recommends.
#
# READ THIS BEFORE CHANGING EITHER FILE. shadowfetch-defaults already Depends
# on shadowfetch-welcome (shadowfetch-workbench reads this package's catalog
# records and runs its bundle installer), so the two packages are now a
# Depends CYCLE. That is deliberate and it is the cheaper of the two evils:
# the alternative was to let the first-boot wizard depend on
# shadowfetch-control-center, which already Recommends this package, and that
# inverts the pillar order. Neither package has a maintainer script that needs
# the other configured first, so dpkg breaks the cycle in either order without
# consequence. The cycle disappears the day the bundle catalog and
# /usr/libexec/shadowfetch-bundle-install move out of this package.
 shadowfetch-defaults (= ${binary:Version})
# The Ignition model cards are rendered from the hwscan fact file, and Welcome
# runs /usr/libexec/shadowfetch-hwscan itself to produce one. shadowfetch-hwscan
# names this package as a consumer in its own description; nothing named it
# back. Recommends, not Depends: Welcome's other pages work without it.
Recommends:
 shadowfetch-hwscan
Description: Shadowfetch first-boot welcome experience
 A simple PyQt6 application launched on first login that walks the user
 through accent color selection, optional Flatpak application installs,
 and other first-run setup.
